Z||000003||voyagerspe||10-03-2001||11:44 PM||||you have a bad pump change it.<P>Pumps that are in the tank cool themselves with the gas that surrounds them.  Ive heard of pumps that run fine as long as the gas tank is more then half full, but if you get down to the 1/4 mark they quit untill you give them a chance to cool down.||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroMaster88||10-03-2001||11:57 PM||||Yep...that sounds like a bad pump.  They get warm and dont work until they cool off.  It sucks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">              <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroHarry||10-04-2001||12:03 AM||||Do you guys see what im getting at about the return line though?<P>How can it run for weeks then after sitting in heavy traffic with "low" flow stop working.<BR>Shouldnt the regulator dump off the excess pressure at idle back thru the return line to allow the pump to have sufficiant flow for cooling?<BR>All pumps have to have flow for cooling or they will overheat.....||||reg||5
Z||000006||maryjane||10-04-2001||01:28 AM||||The way I see it: Sitting in trafic for long periods allows the fuel to become hotter due the high temp of the eng compartment. Return fuel conducts this heat back to the tank and the pump isn't kept as cool. When you are at highway speeds, the eng compartment isn't as hot due to increased air flow thru the vents, and the eng probably isn't either. You said it quit at a gas station once. Was the fuel level low both times it quit? Less volume dissipates heat less. I see what you are saying about a return not working tho. Pumps still turning the same speed, but not much circulation. Seems like it would manifest itself as high fuel pump pressure at the fuel rail tho. Other possibility is the fp relay fails when hot, but the oil pressure switch should keep the pump running even if the fp relay fails.<BR>Don<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 10-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Capt Fiero||10-04-2001||02:33 AM||||Guys how come no one has even mentioned the fuel pump relay.  If the relay gets hot, then as soon as he shuts the car off, and goes to restart it, the relay may not kick the pump back on.  I am not sure what wires you need to jump but I know that you can jump some wires to get the pump on and bypass the relay the next time the problem comes up.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Alex4mula||10-04-2001||08:32 AM||||What brand is the fuel pump? Just in case make sure it is an AC Delco. Replacement brands are pure crap. Once put one that failed in less then 20K miles. That's how I learned to take the tank out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||kyote||10-04-2001||09:28 AM||||Same thing happened to my brothers 86 2.8..<BR>When it was cold it ran fine.. if he went around the block and shut it off.. it wouldn't start.. as long as it was running and not shut off.. it ran fine..<BR>but as soon as he turned it off and came back a few minutes later.. no start..<BR>He changed out the "new" fuel pump he had put in a couple a weeks before..<BR>and now no problem..<P>Change the pump..<P>[QUOTE]Originally posted by FieroHarry:<BR>[B]During my trip home from RFTH6 I got stuck in a traffic jam, then completed a 1.5 hour run @ 85MPH.<BR>When I stopped for gas the fuel pump refused to start.<P> sat for at least three hours) it decideds to run again.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfixit58||10-04-2001||09:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Capt Fiero:<BR><B>Guys how come no one has even mentioned the fuel pump relay.  ....  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I agree with the Capt! Change the relay. They are cheap. Or, the next time it refuses to start, swap the two relays on the firewall. If the relay is sticking, exchanging the relays will cure the pump problem. Then you'll know which relay is bad.<P>The only way it would be your pump is if you starve it of fuel by running the gas tank too low. If you get into that bad habit, you pump will just plain lock up... period.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Hank is Here||10-04-2001||09:34 AM||||I am not even sure if it is possible on today's high pressure systems but is vapor lock an outside possiblity?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||10-04-2001||10:37 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Capt Fiero:<BR><B>Guys how come no one has even mentioned the fuel pump relay.  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If it was the fuel pump relay, shouldn't the oil pressure switch turn on the pump, once the pressure builds?<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000013||mrfixit58||10-04-2001||12:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B> If it was the fuel pump relay, shouldn't the oil pressure switch turn on the pump, once the pressure builds?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The relay is one of the two mounted on the firewall. I believe the oil pressure switch is in series with this relay. It works like this: oil pressure switch detects oil pressure (turns on) and supplies closing voltage to the relay. The fail safe is if the pressure drops out, the relay looses voltage and drops the fuel pump out. The contacts on the relay could be bad.<P>Roy<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||Voytek||10-04-2001||01:49 PM||||ACTUALLY, the oil pressure switch and the relay have two different functions.  I have a bad pressure switch in my '87 GT (have to take out the pump fuse every time I park).<P>So this is what happens: I can hear the relay work properly (it kicks in then out, just the way it should).  BUT, just to make sure, I've swapped this relay with A/C relay (same thing).  No change.<P>The way this works, the relay turns on the pump.  Then the oil pressure switch turns it off once the pressure has been built up.<P>I'm not 100% sure if one can work without the other, but I have heard that the oil pressure switch should turn on the pump IF the relay fails.<P>This probably doesn't help too much - just food for thought.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfiero||10-04-2001||02:46 PM||||This very thing happened with my current '88 GT.  It was fine until I drove for long periods of time in heavy traffic, then it pooped out.  The fuel pump was only a year old (replaced by previous owner), so I didn';t think that was it.<P>I initially thought it was the fuel lines, but after replacing everything but the pump, it still pooped out on long drives.<P>Replace the pump, but replace it with an AC Delco unit......they are more reliable.  The one year old pump that I replaced was an off brand and obviously wasn't up to the task.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Fallman||10-04-2001||08:37 PM||||Same here on my 86 GT.<P>Im replacing pump, and filter. i may have to replace the relay's too but i hope not. i got it to low on gas.||||reg||1
Z||000017||FieroHarry||10-04-2001||08:41 PM||||Thanks for all the info to everyone!<BR>Rest assured the relay works fine, I also have a bypass conection hooked to a switch under my dash.<BR>This was there incase the relay ever fails.<BR>Neither of them would start the pump back up.<P>Some of you mentioned vapor lock, sounds nice but in theory isnt the trouble.<BR>Vapor lock caused by heat would flash the fuel in the lines to a vapor making the engine impossible to run.<BR>Vapor lock would not stop the pump from running, only the pump getting hot.<P>I have to agree with Maryjanes idea over heat and low flow of cooling air.<BR>When this happened the first time I was well over 500 miles from home, after the cooling period it ran all the way there with many gas stops in between.<BR>Always shutting of the car and re-starting with no problems.<BR>(remember though no traffic jams, hence plenty of cooling air flow)<P>The car never really dies on its own like vapor lock.<BR>Just refuses to re-start after I shut it off because the pump wont run.......<BR><P>------------------<BR><BR><B>"If you want to blend in, take the bus!"</B>||||reg||1
Z||000018||maryjane||10-04-2001||09:08 PM||||Here is the scanned diagram. The fuel pump relay is triggered by the ecm, which will send power to the coil in the relay as long as it is receiving ign pulse. Additionally, the oil press switch will send power to the <BR>fuel pump directly anytime the contacts in the oil press switch are closed (above 4psi).<BR>As seen in the diagram, The fuel pump relay circuit can be said to be in parallel with the oil pump switch, but not in series. I see nothing that would indicate that the oil pump switch would kick out the fuel pump relay. Maybe I'm reading it wrong.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fuelinj.jpg">  ||||reg||1
